oesn't.**Reviewing span behaviors and wrapping**\n\nIt looks like spans are not ending where I thought; the outer finally block seems to handle that if it's not a generator. I noticed they've added the capture_output parameter to the _wrap_* functions and wrapper classes, and updated the calls in async_wrapper and sync_wrapper to include it. So now, when they return wrappers, they also pass capture_output to methods that require it.\n\nAdditionally, in the definitions of _wrap_sync_generator_result and _wrap_async_generator_result, they are calling _ContextPreserved*GeneratorWrapper with capture_output included as well, expanding the functionality.**Identifying potential bugs and issues**\n\nI'm observing several potential issues in the updated code. The class's __init__ now includes the capture_output parameter. One concern is that wrapper classes refer to self.span, which might create confusion since it's also a parameter. \n\nIf an exception occurs during iteration, especially for StreamingResponse, there might be a risk of spans remaining open if the body_iterator is never consumed. This behavior can lead to leaks, which may need to be flagged. I'm also wondering if introducing final checks on consumption will suffice or if we should implement a __del__ method for proper span closure. \n\nMoreover, the wrapper classes now call self.span.end() but may not adequately handle exceptions, specifically in the synchronous case, which raises further questions for thread safety and error reporting clarity.  I\u2019ll need to keep all these details in mind while flagging them for potential defects.**Identifying potential findings and issues**\n\nThe diff shows approximate line numbers for functions, making it tricky to pinpoint exact locations in the new file. The start of _handle_observe_result appears around line 479, which highlights a potential issue. Specifically, spans tied to generator consumption are now wrapped, deferring the end of spans when capture_output is F